    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Knowing how to price your home in Seattle is the most important decision you will make as a seller. Price shapes who sees your listing, how many buyers tour it, and whether offers arrive in the first week or the listing quietly sits. The goal is not to pick the highest number you can imagine. It is to set a price that reflects real market evidence, attracts the right buyers quickly, and positions you to negotiate from strength. Here is how experienced Seattle sellers and their agents approach it.</p>

<h2>Start With a Comparative Market Analysis</h2>
<p>Every solid pricing decision starts with a comparative market analysis, or CMA. Your agent pulls data on homes similar to yours and compares them on the details buyers care about.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recently sold homes:</strong> The most important comparisons, because they show what buyers actually paid.</li>
<li><strong>Pending sales:</strong> These show where the market is heading, even before final prices are public.</li>
<li><strong>Active listings:</strong> Your competition. Buyers will tour these alongside your home.</li>
<li><strong>Expired or withdrawn listings:</strong> Useful clues about prices the market rejected.</li>
</ul>
<p>Good comparisons are close in location, size, age, style, lot, and condition. In Seattle, micro-location matters a lot. A home with a view, alley access, or a walk to light rail can differ meaningfully from one a few blocks away. Online estimates rarely capture those nuances, so treat them as a starting point, not a verdict. The same goes for your assessed value from <a href="https://kingcounty.gov/">King County</a>: it is calculated for property tax purposes and is not the same as what a buyer will pay today.</p>

<h2>How to Price Your Home in Seattle: Choosing a Strategy</h2>
<p>Once you know the likely value range, the next question is where to set the list price within or around it. There are two common approaches.</p>
<h3>Pricing at Market Value</h3>
<p>Listing right at the expected value sends a clear, credible signal. Buyers see a fair price and can write confidently. This approach often works well when inventory is higher, when your home is unique and hard to compare, or when you prefer a steady process over a bidding contest.</p>
<h3>Pricing Slightly Under to Draw Offers</h3>
<p>Some Seattle sellers list a bit below expected value to widen the pool of interested buyers. More showings can mean more competition, and competition can push the final price up. This strategy tends to work best when demand is strong and inventory is tight. The risk is real, though: if fewer buyers show up than expected, you may receive offers close to your list price. Only choose this approach if you would be comfortable at the number on the listing.</p>
<blockquote>Whatever strategy you choose, avoid overpricing. A home that starts too high often sits, and buyers notice. Price reductions later can leave sellers with less than they would have earned with a well-calibrated price on day one.</blockquote>

<h2>Using an Offer Review Date</h2>
<p>In many Seattle neighborhoods, sellers set an offer review date, usually several days after the listing goes live. Instead of responding to offers as they come in, you review them together on that date.</p>
<p>The benefits include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Giving buyers time to tour, arrange financing, and review disclosures.</li>
<li>Creating a natural deadline that encourages buyers to put their best terms forward.</li>
<li>Letting you compare offers on price, financing, contingencies, and timing side by side.</li>
</ul>
<p>Sellers can also choose to accept a pre-emptive offer before the review date if it is compelling. Discuss in advance how you want to handle that scenario.</p>

<h2>Why Pre-Inspections Matter Here</h2>
<p>Pre-listing inspections are common in the Seattle area. You hire an inspector before listing and make the report available to buyers. Buyers can then write offers with more confidence, and some may feel comfortable waiving or shortening their own inspection contingency.</p>
<p>A pre-inspection can also help you price more accurately. If it reveals a roof near the end of its life, crawlspace moisture, or an older electrical panel, you can decide whether to fix the issue before listing or account for it in your price. For many Pacific Northwest homes, items like gutters, drainage, and moss on the roof are worth addressing before photos. You will find seasonal maintenance tips on our <a href="/blog/">blog</a>.</p>

<h2>Reading Days on Market Signals</h2>
<p>The first week or two on market tells you a lot. Watch these signals closely with your agent:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Signal</th><th>What It May Mean</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Heavy showings and multiple offers by review date</td><td>Price and presentation resonated with buyers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strong showings but no offers</td><td>Buyers like the home but may see the price as high, or have concerns about condition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Few showings in the first week</td><td>Price may be out of step with the market, or online presentation needs work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Feedback mentioning the same issue</td><td>A fixable problem or a pricing adjustment may help</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If the market is not responding, act quickly. A thoughtful price adjustment early is usually better than waiting several weeks while the listing grows stale.</p>

<h2>Presentation Supports Your Price</h2>
<p>Price and presentation work together. Clean, decluttered rooms, fresh paint in neutral tones, professional photography, and good lighting help buyers see the value you are asking them to pay. In a city with long gray winters, bright, airy photos make a real difference. Small repairs and curb appeal improvements can also help your home compare favorably to the active listings buyers will see the same weekend.</p>

<h2>Timing and Local Context</h2>
<p>Market conditions shift with interest rates, inventory, and the season. Spring is traditionally busy in Seattle, but well-priced homes sell throughout the year, and listing during a quieter stretch can mean less competition. Your agent should update your CMA right before you list so the price reflects current conditions, not those from a few months ago. You can also browse current <a href="/homes-for-sale/">Seattle homes for sale</a> to see what buyers are comparing against.</p>

<h2>Get a Pricing Plan for Your Home</h2>
